hi there steve kaufman here  today i want to talk about the 80 20  rule in language learning and  what i don't think is useful about the  80 20 rule and what i think is useful  the 80 20 rule this is the pareto  principle that 20 of your activity will  give you 80  of your result  by the way two things to point out here  or to say first of all if you enjoy  these videos about language learning  please subscribe to my channel and  second of all i'm going to at the end of  this discussion show you a brief uh  interview or  portion of my lesson with my one of my  ukrainian tutors  so you can listen to that if you're  interested and i will tell you later on  what i talk about i'm not going to  produce subtitles all right  now a number of people have asked me for  example to tell them what i do in my  discussions with my tutors so i will  explain that a little bit but first  let's get to this idea of the 80 20  principle some people say if you learn a  very small number of words that is going  to help you  learn the language that gives you 80 of  the language i don't believe that all  right  what i do believe is that you should  spend 80 of your time  on  big picture what i call  top-down  listening and reading engaging with the  language doing interesting things in the  language and 20 of your time on the nuts  and bolts which is grammar which is  practicing grammar  so that a very small percentage and in  particular this idea that the you should  learn the most common words of the  language first i mean you can if you  want to but it's not necessary because  those most common words are going to  show up  very often as soon as you start engaging  with the language where i do think it's  useful  is if you whatever language you're  learning if you go and google for the  100 most common verbs in the language  not nouns because that'll depend on your  interest but the verbs in the language  if you master those if you really learn  how they're used  and you won't do that on the first pass  through you won't begin by learning the  100 most common verbs but it's something  that you can go back to all the time and  make sure that you know how to use those  100 most common verbs that i think is  quite useful and i'm starting to do more  and more of that and i think that's for  example where pyotr's simple stories in  polish real polish dot pl are very good  because a lot of his stories go over the  use of the most common  uh  verbs in the language that is very  helpful however  the bulk of my learning activity is  engaging with things that are  interesting as i said earlier i have to  begin with the simple you know 30-second  stuff in the starter books  and and i often i should say i didn't  say that i often go back to the starting  books time and time again because again  it's that 20 of time that you spend with  the nuts and bolts uh which could be  going over again those lessons in the  starter book or listening to simple  stories like pyotr store stories in  polish which i do from time to time to  kind of refresh  my grasp of some of the basic verbs and  and things in the language but 80 of my  time is spent on things that are  interesting  and and  that then gets the subject of how i use  my  sessions with my tutors as you know i  wait until i'm able to understand and  that i wait until i have some words  and so then my sessions with my tutors  are simply discussions discussions about  whatever interests us and  if i find that a tutor is not  interesting cannot have maintain a  conversation then i don't continue with  that tutor or occasionally i'll get a  sort of quote professional tutor who  tells me today we're going to do the  numbers  i don't go back to that tutor i'm not  interested in that i'm interested in  having a general discussion after which  the tutor sends me a list of all the  phrases and and words that i had trouble  with and then i studied them and so as  an example of that now  of that 80 percent focus on interesting  things that listen to and read and then  in the discussion again focus on  interesting things  uh so i'm going to show you now a  discussion with katarina who is my tutor  in nepro nippo nipro petrovsk in ukraine  who is a very interesting person and we  have had very interesting discussions on  history she gave me some links to  very interesting his ukrainian  historians she works as a historian as  an archaeologist teaches at the  university she's involved with a  volunteer organization that helps  particularly displaced persons people  displaced by the war in ukraine  helps them obtain grants from  international organizations it helps  them uh set up businesses if they had a  business in donetsk can they move now to  nipro she helps them  get money or other resources that can  help them do that and so we discuss that  in this uh lesson so you're going to see  a few minutes of my ukrainian lesson  with catherine and you'll see how  interesting it is to engage with  interesting people in the language and  the actual nuts and bolts of the  language i still have to work on i have  to work on them and i spend 10 15 20 of  my time trying to nail those things down  but that just takes time and  ultimately it's that combination of of  80 doing interesting things in the  language  20  maximum with the nuts and bolts that  eventually enable you to become uh  hopefully  if not fluent at least comfortable in  the language so thank you for listening  and now if you want you can listen to  the ukrainian discussion with katarina  [Music]  you
 

